1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:18,160
How did Paul Swigga get its name? That is probably the most common question I get asked,

2
00:00:18,160 --> 00:00:25,360
and the short answer is without very much thought at all. So I was starting out as a pen tester,

3
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:31,680
as a computer hacker. And in those days, this was before Twitter, anything like that, to go and

4
00:00:31,680 --> 00:00:37,280
learn and get information, you tended to hang out on forums, bulletin boards, mailing lists,

5
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:43,280
that kind of thing. And you'd often be making posts to ask questions, that sort of thing.

6
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:49,920
Everyone said, you need a handle, don't post under your real name. Computer hacking was a bit

7
00:00:49,920 --> 00:00:56,960
disreputable, people didn't really understand it, didn't even know it was legal. Even your employer,

8
00:00:56,960 --> 00:01:01,360
who was paying you to do this as a job, didn't really want to be associated with it in public.

9
00:01:02,080 --> 00:01:10,240
So they needed a handle. And this was before the modern web. And pen testing was mostly aimed at the

10
00:01:11,120 --> 00:01:16,880
network and the infrastructure layer. And one of the terms you talked about a lot was the term port.

11
00:01:16,880 --> 00:01:23,760
And one of the tools was a port scanner, the scanner network to find open ports. So in about 10 seconds,

12
00:01:23,760 --> 00:01:29,760
I thought, well, port is also a drink. And instead of scanning, you say swigger, port swigger,

13
00:01:29,760 --> 00:01:36,800
is a bit of a silly pun, it means somebody drinks port. And that was it. I didn't expect it to become

14
00:01:37,840 --> 00:01:44,960
known, to become associated with me, to be anything like a business, which it eventually did.

15
00:01:44,960 --> 00:01:52,480
And a long time later, we eventually hired some brand consultants and asked them, you know,

16
00:01:52,480 --> 00:01:58,320
is port swigger, is this the right name? Is this working for us? Is it a bit too silly? Do we need

17
00:01:58,320 --> 00:02:03,600
to grow up? And they went and thought about it. And fortunately, they came back and said, you should

18
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:12,960
keep it because it's different. Everybody else has names like net and sec and tech in their name. And

19
00:02:12,960 --> 00:02:16,880
there's nobody else like port swigger. So at least you stand out, which is what I wanted to hear,

20
00:02:16,880 --> 00:02:18,160
because I wasn't going to change it anyway.

21
00:02:23,760 --> 00:02:30,400
So how did Burps We start? Well, I was a pen tester just getting started,

22
00:02:31,120 --> 00:02:37,200
learning the tricks. I was also lazy, like many pen testers, and I wanted to automate my job.

23
00:02:37,200 --> 00:02:45,040
An awful lot of pen testing is laborious, repetitive, error prone. You just want to find a way to speed

24
00:02:45,040 --> 00:02:50,880
it up. It isn't that much fun. And there weren't any good tools around, particularly for web testing.

25
00:02:50,880 --> 00:03:00,800
So I decided to write my own. My initial focus was on automating customer tax. So I had ideas for

26
00:03:01,440 --> 00:03:08,000
placing payloads into an HTTP request in different places, iterating over different types of payloads,

27
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:14,000
sending all the requests quickly, capturing results. And that was the first decent web testing tool I

28
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:20,240
wrote, and I called it Burp. And that eventually evolved into Burp Intruder. And then along the way,

29
00:03:20,240 --> 00:03:27,040
I also wrote a proxy, a spider, repeater tool, various other bits and pieces, and put them all together.

30
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:28,960
And that became Burp Suite.

31
00:03:28,960 --> 00:03:41,200
So I did Burp Suite get its name for no real good reason. So I was in those days, I was in the habit of

32
00:03:41,200 --> 00:03:46,800
writing disposable tools every few days, start on work on some of the little tool or other,

33
00:03:47,360 --> 00:03:53,840
run with it for a bit, get bored, do something else, and I tend to give them all random names

34
00:03:53,840 --> 00:03:58,800
without any thought at all. And one day I was working on this tool and I called it Burp. And

35
00:03:58,800 --> 00:04:04,480
that just ended up being the one that I ran with and carried on working for years and years. I had

36
00:04:04,480 --> 00:04:08,880
no idea at the time I started that it would last longer than any of the others, but it did.

37
00:04:14,880 --> 00:04:22,080
So I've Burp written in Java, more or less by accident. You probably see a pattern emerging here.

38
00:04:22,080 --> 00:04:30,000
I was a hacker who could code. I wasn't a real software developer. I'd never worked with anybody

39
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,280
else and never had any training. When I started learning to code, I taught myself C, C++. And then

40
00:04:37,280 --> 00:04:44,960
when I wanted to make tools with a user interface, I started using C Sharp. C Sharp was the new shiny

41
00:04:44,960 --> 00:04:52,000
thing then and seemed pretty good. Worked with that for a while. One day, I think I got a new laptop

42
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:58,080
at work and I ended up losing my copy of Visual Studio. Visual Studio wasn't free back then. You

43
00:04:58,080 --> 00:05:02,640
had to pay for it. And I think I originally installed it off some MSDN disks that were lying around the

44
00:05:02,640 --> 00:05:08,720
office and I couldn't find them. So I went and I think I went and try to download a pirate copy and

45
00:05:08,720 --> 00:05:15,040
couldn't find all the bits. And anyway, I was too tight to pay up for an IDE. Eventually, after a couple of

46
00:05:15,040 --> 00:05:20,160
days, I got a bit frustrated and just thought, well, you know what? Isn't Java the same as C Sharp? I'll

47
00:05:20,160 --> 00:05:25,680
just maybe try Java next to my next language. Downloaded NetBeans. NetBeans is free. Got up and

48
00:05:25,680 --> 00:05:33,120
running about an hour later. I was writing my first Java tool. Actually, in C Sharp, I'd already built

49
00:05:33,120 --> 00:05:39,600
a simple tool that was similar in concept to Burp Intruder. And the first thing I did when I got

50
00:05:39,600 --> 00:05:45,040
started with Java was to just recreate that tool because I thought it looked interesting and I wanted

51
00:05:45,040 --> 00:05:52,240
to carry on working on it. So I just started again in Java and that ended up being Burp. And so Burp

52
00:05:52,240 --> 00:06:00,320
was actually my hello world program in the Java language. So today we have real developers who know

53
00:06:00,320 --> 00:06:05,600
what they're doing, working on the code base. And if any of them look at some of the really oldest

54
00:06:05,600 --> 00:06:10,480
code, I wonder why it's in such a mass. It was because it was literally the first code I ever wrote

55
00:06:10,480 --> 00:06:23,040
in Java. When did I realize that Burp Suite could be a successful business? Well, for a long time,

56
00:06:23,040 --> 00:06:30,640
for many years, it was just a pure labor of love. I was just doing it for fun. I enjoyed thinking about

57
00:06:30,640 --> 00:06:36,560
difficult problems, building solutions to them, writing code and sharing what I built with other

58
00:06:36,560 --> 00:06:43,840
people. It also made my job more efficient steadily over time. So I got this virtuous circle going where

59
00:06:43,840 --> 00:06:49,120
I'd spend some time working on Burp, write a feature that made me more efficient so I could do my job

60
00:06:49,120 --> 00:06:53,920
quicker. So I had a bit more time to work on Burp. But that was just really good fun for ages.

61
00:06:54,640 --> 00:06:59,680
Burp was doing pretty well. It was nowhere near enough money to live off, but it was just,

62
00:06:59,680 --> 00:07:08,080
it was a really nice little hobby to have. And then one day, we did some consultancy for a huge

63
00:07:08,080 --> 00:07:15,040
software company who were considering buying a web vulnerability scanner. And they asked us to look

64
00:07:15,040 --> 00:07:20,800
at all of the scanners that were out there and assess them to see how good they were. And I was

65
00:07:20,800 --> 00:07:27,680
leading that job. And so I did that. My conclusion was, these scanners really suck.

66
00:07:27,680 --> 00:07:34,240
All kinds of bugs that were really commonplace. You ran into all the time, pen testing,

67
00:07:34,240 --> 00:07:39,600
they didn't find them. So I just got to thinking, well, seriously, how hard can it be

68
00:07:40,080 --> 00:07:46,160
to write a half decent web vulnerability scanner? So I decided to do it and build my own.

69
00:07:46,160 --> 00:07:54,480
A few months later, it went into Burp Suite as a scanner. And literally overnight, that put a zero

70
00:07:54,480 --> 00:08:00,080
on our revenue. Everyone, loads of pen testers are lazy. They love the idea of having a vulnerability

71
00:08:00,080 --> 00:08:06,320
scanner built into their toolkit. And it was really successful. And that made me think, well, I can

72
00:08:06,320 --> 00:08:13,840
probably live off this. So soon enough, quit my job. I was able to put all my time into Burp.

73
00:08:13,840 --> 00:08:17,840
And eventually start to build the team behind it. And never look back.

74
00:08:17,840 --> 00:08:29,440
So what's my favourite Burp Suite feature? There are probably two answers to that. There's an answer

75
00:08:29,440 --> 00:08:35,040
from the heart and an answer from the head. And the answer from the heart is it's probably the manual

76
00:08:35,040 --> 00:08:43,440
testing simulator. So this was a really silly joke feature that I put into Burp a long time ago. I

77
00:08:43,440 --> 00:08:47,440
can't even remember writing it all. Why? I must've just been bored one day and it was a bit of fun.

78
00:08:48,160 --> 00:08:53,600
And what it basically does, it simulates the activity of a manual tester by sending traffic to

79
00:08:53,600 --> 00:08:59,680
the website that you're attacking, you're targeting, just to make you look busy. And the idea was if you're

80
00:08:59,680 --> 00:09:04,720
on a, on a pen test and you need to take some time off to have a lunch or meet a friend or go to the gym,

81
00:09:04,720 --> 00:09:10,000
where you just leave this running. And if anyone's looking at the firewall logs or the server logs,

82
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:15,680
it'll just look like you're, you're still busy. So just released this for a bit of fun. It turned

83
00:09:15,680 --> 00:09:22,160
out quite a lot of people are using it. We had, we get occasional bug reports from users who really

84
00:09:22,160 --> 00:09:25,760
missed the point. They said they've been running the manual testing simulator all day and it hasn't

85
00:09:25,760 --> 00:09:33,520
reported any issues. We occasionally, one time we got say a Burp customer contact us to say they'd

86
00:09:33,520 --> 00:09:38,720
engaged a pen tester to test their website and they were looking at the server logs and they could

87
00:09:38,720 --> 00:09:44,240
tell he was just running the manual testing simulator. And they wanted us to look at the logs and confirm

88
00:09:44,240 --> 00:09:49,600
this so they could take it up with them. And we kind of said, well, we probably shouldn't get involved.

89
00:09:49,600 --> 00:09:55,360
This is between between you and him. So that's the kind of, that's the fun feature that I think is

90
00:09:55,360 --> 00:10:00,160
the coolest. The more serious answer, the answer from the head is definitely Burp collaborator.

91
00:10:00,160 --> 00:10:06,960
So Burp collaborator is absolutely incredible. It's, it finds invisible bugs. It finds asynchronous

92
00:10:06,960 --> 00:10:13,280
vulnerabilities that are already triggered after you finished your testing. It just finds issues

93
00:10:13,280 --> 00:10:20,320
that are completely undetectable in the application's behavior that you can observe as a, directly as a

94
00:10:20,320 --> 00:10:26,480
tester or as a scanner. This really was a game changer. So traditionally there's just been this

95
00:10:26,480 --> 00:10:31,760
dichotomy between different approaches to scanning. So SAST or static application security testing,

96
00:10:32,400 --> 00:10:37,680
traditionally people say, well, you know, it has high coverage. It finds a lot of bugs. It also has

97
00:10:38,240 --> 00:10:44,160
huge number of false positives, generates a lot of noise. Whereas DAST, dynamic scanning has lower

98
00:10:44,160 --> 00:10:48,080
coverage, doesn't find as many bugs, but then it tends to be more accurate. It doesn't have as many

99
00:10:48,640 --> 00:10:54,640
false positives. You take your choice. Well, Burp collaborator, we call it OAST, which is

100
00:10:54,640 --> 00:11:01,200
out-of-band application security testing. This has way better coverage than DAST because it can see

101
00:11:01,200 --> 00:11:06,240
inside the application and it can trigger vulnerabilities that have no visible effect

102
00:11:06,240 --> 00:11:13,600
on the application's behavior, but it's also pretty much free of false positives. So if we send a payload

103
00:11:13,600 --> 00:11:20,560
to exploit, say, SQL injection containing an obfuscated domain name, if something on the server side does a DNS

104
00:11:20,560 --> 00:11:26,240
lookup on that domain name, we can be pretty certain that that payload was processed in a SQL query and

105
00:11:26,240 --> 00:11:32,480
that it was vulnerable. So it's a fantastic step forward in the whole paradigm of dynamic scanning.

106
00:11:33,040 --> 00:11:37,920
So that's the real answer. The overall coolest feature of Burp Suite, I would say, is Burp collaborator.

107
00:11:37,920 --> 00:11:49,760
So who is Peter Weiner? Well, he's actually a good friend of mine. It's not quite his real name.

108
00:11:50,720 --> 00:11:57,360
He's a guy who, he's a pen tester. Many years ago, we were working together on site and he went to

109
00:11:57,920 --> 00:12:02,720
up to a door and buzzed the intercom to be let into the building and said his name. And the

110
00:12:02,720 --> 00:12:08,800
person on the other hand, other and misheard and they said, Peter Weiner. And we just had a bit of a

111
00:12:08,800 --> 00:12:14,960
laugh about that. And it just stuck as a nickname. And we started to call him it. A bit later, I was

112
00:12:14,960 --> 00:12:20,720
working on the Burp spider feature where you would submit forms and send inputs. And when I needed a

113
00:12:20,720 --> 00:12:26,720
name, I just decided to use Peter Weiner, half as a little tribute to him, but just mostly to wind him up a bit,

114
00:12:26,720 --> 00:12:33,360
bit of a joke. Peter Weiner went everywhere because Burp would submit this all the time when it was

115
00:12:33,360 --> 00:12:39,680
when it was spidering and scanning, there were reports of complete mayhem. People with printers

116
00:12:39,680 --> 00:12:45,600
where Peter Weiner's name was showing on the LCD screen of the printer. Loads of people who's helped

