129
00:14:01,680 --> 00:14:08,240
So is it going to be a third edition of WA? Tons of readers have asked for this, and the publisher

130
00:14:08,240 --> 00:14:14,800
absolutely has asked for it. The short answer is no, there isn't. There are some really big downsides

131
00:14:14,800 --> 00:14:22,320
of writing a book. You do a ton of work all at once. It starts about dates. It's not interactive.

132
00:14:22,320 --> 00:14:27,920
People can't discover it easily through search. And basically, I couldn't face doing it all over again.

133
00:14:29,200 --> 00:14:36,560
So instead of a third edition of WA, we made the Web Security Academy. And this is online, it's digital,

134
00:14:36,560 --> 00:14:44,160
it's interactive, it's completely free for everyone. It's constantly updated. We have over 150 vulnerability

135
00:14:44,160 --> 00:14:51,440
labs where you can learn how to find real vulnerabilities safely and legally. So to anyone

136
00:14:51,440 --> 00:14:58,080
still reading the Web Appakers handbook, now I would say this is the time to switch and discover the Web

137
00:14:58,080 --> 00:15:10,240
Security Academy. What do we have against Carlos? If you don't know who Carlos is, in the Web Security

138
00:15:10,240 --> 00:15:16,880
Academy, he is this person who appears in many of the labs. And the challenge to complete the lab is to

139
00:15:16,880 --> 00:15:22,560
delete Carlos. And this often involves something like compromising an administrator's account, taking

140
00:15:22,560 --> 00:15:30,800
control of the application and deleting the Carlos user. But seriously, Carlos is known to us in real life.

141
00:15:31,760 --> 00:15:41,520
He's a very sinister individual. He's perhaps the epitome of pure evil. That's why we need to keep deleting him.

142
00:15:41,520 --> 00:15:55,360
So how did I get into Web Security without a real plan to do so? When I was at uni, I studied philosophy.

143
00:15:55,920 --> 00:16:02,080
I was there stuck around a long time. I was there eight years. I did a PhD. I was doing lecturing. I was

144
00:16:02,080 --> 00:16:11,120
really considering a career in academia. And then I got a bit bored of it. So I needed a job. As a child,

145
00:16:11,120 --> 00:16:17,600
I'd always played around with computers. I taught myself to program in basic on things like ZX81, ZX

146
00:16:17,600 --> 00:16:24,480
Spectrum. So I thought, well, I'll find something I can do with computers. So my first job was a general

147
00:16:24,480 --> 00:16:32,800
IT consultant. And I learned a lot about tech, but it wasn't too exciting. A lot of things like project

148
00:16:32,800 --> 00:16:40,000
management, audits, security policy stuff, things like that. Then one day, I got to know some people at

149
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:44,800
the company who were doing this thing called penetration testing or computer hacking. And

150
00:16:44,800 --> 00:16:52,480
this sounded way more fun. I managed to get onto a training course they were running. I taught myself

151
00:16:52,480 --> 00:16:58,720
a lot of things as well. I started playing around, writing bits of tools to help me. Eventually, I got

152
00:16:58,720 --> 00:17:06,560
on some actual pen testing jobs they were doing. And that was my big break. I absolutely loved it. I loved

153
00:17:06,560 --> 00:17:14,000
breaking into things. I loved being devious. I loved the kick you get when you own something. It was full

154
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:19,360
of interesting challenges. I just really loved building things, solving problems, sharing, sharing

155
00:17:19,360 --> 00:17:24,960
the tools that I built to do so. It ticked every box for me and I never looked back.

156
00:17:24,960 --> 00:17:37,440
What was my advice to someone getting started as a penetration tester? Well, expect to work hard.

157
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:43,840
Expect to teach yourself an awful lot. I would say today, the Web Security Academy is the best

158
00:17:43,840 --> 00:17:50,080
educational resource for learning web security and hacking. So I would start there. I would learn to code.

159
00:17:50,080 --> 00:17:56,160
I would, you can write your own tools. You can customize other tools. You can use the Burp API to

160
00:17:56,160 --> 00:18:03,920
write amazing Burp extensions to do crazy things. Writing code means you understand how developers

161
00:18:03,920 --> 00:18:09,040
think and the kind of mistakes they can make. And that really helps you as a hacker when you're trying

162
00:18:09,040 --> 00:18:15,600
to break in. I would spend a lot of time on bug bounty sites. Years ago, when I started, the only way you

163
00:18:15,600 --> 00:18:20,880
could legally test a site for vulnerabilities was on a paid engagement where they're giving you approval.

164
00:18:21,440 --> 00:18:26,000
These days, there are thousands of sites on the web that let anybody come and hack them and they will

165
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:32,400
pay you for the bugs that you find. Go to sites like HackerOne to find good bug bounty sites to work on.

166
00:18:33,040 --> 00:18:38,880
I would also read the public reports of bug bounties. There are masses of write-ups of vulnerabilities

167
00:18:38,880 --> 00:18:43,680
where you can see how other hackers have found them and the evidence they got and the methodology they used.

168
00:18:43,680 --> 00:18:49,840
I would also form a network, go to events, make friends in the industry, share ideas with them.

169
00:18:50,560 --> 00:18:56,320
But mainly, work hard, keep learning. Web security is a huge area, but it is all great fun.

170
00:19:01,440 --> 00:19:07,920
So what is life like at Portswiger? Well, it's not like anywhere else, certainly anywhere else that I've worked.

171
00:19:07,920 --> 00:19:16,240
Portswiger has been shaped by the formative experiences that I had in the places I worked before my career.

172
00:19:16,240 --> 00:19:24,800
So my first job was in a huge accountancy firm. It was extremely hierarchical, very tightly controlled,

173
00:19:24,800 --> 00:19:31,520
full of huge egos, everyone driven by status. It really wasn't my thing. I then moved to a tiny

174
00:19:31,520 --> 00:19:38,480
pen testing firm, which by comparison was total chaos. It was great fun. I learned a lot from some

175
00:19:38,480 --> 00:19:45,600
amazing people. Eventually, they kind of grew a bit too fast and started to lower the bar on who they

176
00:19:45,600 --> 00:19:52,000
hired and it ended up not quite as good as it had been. They then got acquired by a really big pen testing

177
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:57,840
company that was a listed business, obsessed with money and financial targets and basically everything

178
00:19:57,840 --> 00:20:07,920
changed. So when it came time to build the Portswiger team, I was absolutely fixated on avoiding mistakes

179
00:20:07,920 --> 00:20:14,480
that I think I'd seen elsewhere. So for me, the most important thing was hiring exceptional people. So

180
00:20:14,480 --> 00:20:21,600
we have an amazing diverse team. We have loads of software engineers, we have researchers, we have product

